WEBVTT

00:00:04.780 --> 00:00:10.380
Achieving a perfect score on your federal Medicare ratings used to be a bit like taking your car

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:13.580
in for a standard annual inspection, you know?

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:16.780
Right, like the mechanic checks the oil, kicks the tires.

00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:21.500
Yeah, exactly. They make sure the brake lights work, and I mean, as long as nothing is actively

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:24.140
on fire, you get your sticker and you're good for another year.

00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:25.900
Pretty low bar, all things considered.

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:30.060
Right, but imagine pulling into that exact same inspection bay today,

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:32.540
and the mechanic tells you the rules have completely changed.

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:36.380
Now they require your car to have this, like, real-time digital dashboard,

00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:41.260
tracking tire pressure, transmission fluid, and brake pad wear, all simultaneously.

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:45.980
Oh, and they are absolutely going to penalize you if a warning light flashes for even a second

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:47.420
while you're out on the highway.

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:51.820
Exactly. And that sudden, just uncompromising shift in difficulty?

00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:55.020
Well, that is exactly what the United States government just did to the Medicare

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:56.620
Advantage grading system.

00:00:56.620 --> 00:01:00.140
Yeah, the metrics for success didn't just inch upward.

00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:06.060
The whole underlying philosophy of what constitutes good healthcare was just completely rewritten.

00:01:06.620 --> 00:01:08.460
Welcome to today's Deep Dive.

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:12.620
Our mission today is to cut through the incredibly dense, often overwhelming

00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:14.860
bureaucracy of the U.S. healthcare system.

00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:16.380
Which is no small task.

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:17.180
No kidding.

00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:20.700
But we want to find out exactly what it takes to be considered a perfect

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:23.020
health plan right now in the year 2027.

00:01:23.660 --> 00:01:26.620
We're going to explore why so many massive organizations are suddenly

00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:31.660
failing this test. And more importantly, who is actually passing it?

00:01:31.660 --> 00:01:33.900
We are so glad you're joining us for this one.

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:39.580
To map out this shifting landscape, we're drawing from a really specific stack of sources today.

00:01:39.580 --> 00:01:44.860
We are looking at the official 2027 Medicare Advantage and Part D Star Ratings fact sheet,

00:01:44.860 --> 00:01:49.340
which is published directly by CMS, the Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services.

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:51.260
Right, the actual grading rubric.

00:01:51.260 --> 00:01:55.020
Exactly. And we're cross-referencing that with an industry data breakdown

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:56.620
from Becker's Payer Issues.

00:01:56.620 --> 00:02:00.860
And to figure out how to actually survive this crazy new regulatory environment,

00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:04.780
we're diving into some incredible local reporting out of Puerto Rico from El Nuevo

00:02:04.780 --> 00:02:06.380
Día and Sin Comillas.

00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:11.740
Because when you put all of those sources together, you uncover this like central mystery.

00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:15.660
Right. In a year where Medicare Advantage ratings are just plummeting all across the

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:19.900
mainland United States, how is Puerto Rico managing to crack the code?

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:26.140
I mean, how are they delivering top-tier five-star care to hundreds of thousands of patients

00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:29.260
under some of the toughest economic conditions in the entire country?

00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:33.900
It's a massive statistical anomaly, and it's forcing the entire industry to pay attention.

00:02:34.460 --> 00:02:38.380
But to truly understand how unprecedented their success is,

00:02:38.380 --> 00:02:41.260
we first need to establish the baseline, right?

00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:45.500
Yeah, we have to look at the national battlefield that CMS just created for 2027.

00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:48.380
Like, what do these star ratings actually measure?

00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:51.500
And why did the test just get so much harder?

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:53.340
Okay, let's unpack this.

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:58.140
For anyone who, you know, doesn't spend their days reading federal health care policy,

00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:01.100
Medicare evaluates over 500 plans every year.

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:05.980
Specifically, I think it was 508 contracts that got rated this cycle.

00:03:05.980 --> 00:03:09.820
Right. And they aren't just sending out a survey asking if you liked the

00:03:09.820 --> 00:03:11.500
magazines in your doctor's waiting room.

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:15.180
They use up to 43 unique performance measures

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:17.820
for these Medicare Advantage prescription drug contracts.

00:03:18.540 --> 00:03:22.220
Wow, 43. That's a lot of metrics.

00:03:22.780 --> 00:03:26.780
It's up to 43 distinct, highly rigorous metrics.

00:03:26.780 --> 00:03:31.420
I mean, they are evaluating everything from how effectively a plan manages chronic conditions,

00:03:31.420 --> 00:03:36.780
like diabetes and hypertension, to the quality and frequency of preventive screenings.

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:39.980
Oh, and I saw they even measure customer service efficiency.

00:03:39.980 --> 00:03:43.900
Appeal win rates, wait times for appointments, and wait,

00:03:43.900 --> 00:03:47.260
the availability of foreign language interpreters at call centers?

00:03:47.260 --> 00:03:48.540
Yeah, even that.

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:48.860
Wow.

00:03:48.860 --> 00:03:53.500
It's an exhaustive audit of how these organizations operate on a literal daily basis.

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:58.860
And based on all of that data, CMS establishes cut points to determine

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:03.900
if a plan gets one, two, three, four, or the highly coveted five stars.

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:09.980
But when the 2027 ratings dropped, it wasn't just a slight adjustment, was it?

00:04:09.980 --> 00:04:11.020
No, not at all.

00:04:11.580 --> 00:04:14.540
The industry broadly referred to it as a bloodbath.

00:04:14.540 --> 00:04:15.340
A bloodbath.

00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:15.740
Yeah.

00:04:15.740 --> 00:04:16.220
Wow.

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:16.940
Yeah.

00:04:16.940 --> 00:04:19.980
The data definitely reflects a massive downward trend.

00:04:19.980 --> 00:04:23.820
A key metric to look at here is the enrollment weighted average rating.

00:04:23.820 --> 00:04:26.300
Okay, let's define that for the listener real quick.

00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:30.940
The enrollment weighted average isn't just like taking the scores of all 508 plans

00:04:30.940 --> 00:04:32.620
and dividing them by 508.

00:04:32.620 --> 00:04:32.860
Right.

00:04:32.860 --> 00:04:33.900
That would skew the data.

00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:34.620
Exactly.

00:04:34.620 --> 00:04:39.420
It factors in how many actual human beings are enrolled in those plans.

00:04:39.420 --> 00:04:43.740
So a massive plan with 2 million people carries way more weight in this average

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:46.540
than some tiny plan with 5,000 people.

00:04:46.540 --> 00:04:51.100
It basically tells us the average experience of the actual patient population.

00:04:51.100 --> 00:04:53.180
That's exactly the distinction we need.

00:04:53.180 --> 00:04:57.500
And when you look at that enrollment weighted average for Medicare Advantage this year,

00:04:57.500 --> 00:04:59.740
it dropped to 3.99.

00:04:59.740 --> 00:05:07.580
Which, okay, that might not sound like a huge fall from 4.01 in 2026 or 4.07 back in 2024.

00:05:08.140 --> 00:05:11.260
Right, but in the aggregate of millions of patients,

00:05:11.260 --> 00:05:14.300
it represents a massive downgrade in federal scoring.

00:05:14.300 --> 00:05:19.100
I mean, overall, only about 37% of plans earned 4 stars or higher this year.

00:05:19.100 --> 00:05:19.740
Oh, wow.

00:05:19.740 --> 00:05:24.860
Yeah, that is a really steep drop from about 44% in 2026.

00:05:24.860 --> 00:05:28.860
But the real shocker, and honestly the main reason we're doing this deep dive,

00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:31.100
is what happened at the absolute top of the mountain.

00:05:31.820 --> 00:05:38.060
Nationwide, only 15 Medicare Advantage plans earned a perfect 5-star rating for 2027.

00:05:38.060 --> 00:05:40.140
Down from 22 the previous year.

00:05:40.140 --> 00:05:42.860
So the obvious question is, you know, did the health plans just

00:05:42.860 --> 00:05:45.100
suddenly get way worse at doing their jobs?

00:05:45.100 --> 00:05:47.980
The plans didn't necessarily change their behavior at all.

00:05:47.980 --> 00:05:49.580
CMS changed the math.

00:05:49.580 --> 00:05:53.420
They introduced new, like, highly strict clinical measures.

00:05:53.420 --> 00:05:58.460
For instance, they added a measure to track the concurrent use of opioids and benzodiazepines.

00:05:58.460 --> 00:06:01.980
So making sure a patient isn't being prescribed a heavy pain killer

00:06:01.980 --> 00:06:05.180
and a heavy anti-anxiety medication at the exact same time.

00:06:05.180 --> 00:06:08.060
Because I mean, that combination could dangerously suppress breathing, right?

00:06:08.060 --> 00:06:08.780
Correct.

00:06:08.780 --> 00:06:10.220
That's a huge safety issue.

00:06:10.780 --> 00:06:15.660
Another major addition is a measure tracking the use of multiple anticholinergic medications

00:06:15.660 --> 00:06:16.460
in older adults.

00:06:17.100 --> 00:06:19.740
Okay, honestly, I consider myself pretty well read.

00:06:19.740 --> 00:06:22.460
But I totally had to look that one up in our notes.

00:06:22.460 --> 00:06:26.940
What are we actually talking about when we say anticholinergic medications?

00:06:26.940 --> 00:06:29.980
And why is CMS suddenly so laser focused on them?

00:06:29.980 --> 00:06:33.260
It's a perfect example of how CMS is raising the bar,

00:06:33.900 --> 00:06:37.420
moving from simple administrative checks to intense clinical oversight.

00:06:38.060 --> 00:06:42.380
Anticholinergics are a class of drugs that basically blocks certain neurotransmitters

00:06:42.380 --> 00:06:43.180
in the nervous system.

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:45.420
Are these, like, rare drugs?

00:06:46.140 --> 00:06:47.020
Not at all.

00:06:47.020 --> 00:06:49.900
We are talking about incredibly common medications,

00:06:49.900 --> 00:06:53.740
certain over-the-counter antihistamines, specific antidepressants,

00:06:53.740 --> 00:06:55.580
or medications for overactive bladders.

00:06:56.220 --> 00:07:01.580
The danger is that when older adults take multiple medications in this class simultaneously,

00:07:01.580 --> 00:07:05.660
it can cause severe confusion, dizziness, and cognitive impairment that actually

00:07:05.660 --> 00:07:06.780
mimics dementia.

00:07:06.780 --> 00:07:07.980
Wait, really?

00:07:07.980 --> 00:07:12.540
So a patient might end up in a neurologist's office being evaluated for dementia

00:07:12.540 --> 00:07:17.180
when, in reality, they're just suffering from a toxic overlap of totally routine prescriptions?

00:07:17.820 --> 00:07:18.140
Yes.

00:07:18.780 --> 00:07:21.340
That is the hidden danger CMS is trying to spotlight.

00:07:21.900 --> 00:07:25.660
And catching this requires a massive level of coordination,

00:07:25.660 --> 00:07:28.700
because a patient might see, you know, a cardiologist for one issue,

00:07:28.700 --> 00:07:31.900
a psychiatrist for another, and their primary care doctor for a third.

00:07:32.460 --> 00:07:35.820
And those doctors aren't always looking at the exact same digital charts.

00:07:35.820 --> 00:07:36.460
Exactly.

00:07:36.860 --> 00:07:39.180
But the health plan sits above all of them.

00:07:39.820 --> 00:07:41.420
CMS is basically telling the health plans,

00:07:41.420 --> 00:07:43.740
hey, you process all the pharmacy claims.

00:07:43.740 --> 00:07:48.140
It's your job to see the overlap, flag it, and intervene before the patient gets hurt.

00:07:48.140 --> 00:07:51.660
Okay, this totally goes back to that digital dashboard analogy.

00:07:51.660 --> 00:07:53.820
CMS isn't just checking the oil anymore.

00:07:53.820 --> 00:07:57.420
They are demanding a real-time system that tracks all these moving parts

00:07:57.420 --> 00:07:59.900
across entirely different doctors' offices.

00:07:59.900 --> 00:08:03.420
And they penalize the health plan if a dangerous drug combination slips through.

00:08:03.420 --> 00:08:03.660
Right.

00:08:04.300 --> 00:08:06.220
And they didn't just add new measures either.

00:08:06.220 --> 00:08:08.860
They completely changed the grading weight of the existing ones.

00:08:09.500 --> 00:08:12.780
Yeah, that weighting shift is arguably the biggest hurdle of all.

00:08:12.780 --> 00:08:15.980
I saw that CMS tripled the weight of metrics that measure the improvement

00:08:15.980 --> 00:08:18.860
or maintenance of physical and mental health outcomes.

00:08:18.860 --> 00:08:19.420
Tripled it.

00:08:19.980 --> 00:08:24.700
So it went from a weight of one to a weight of three in the overall grading formula.

00:08:25.340 --> 00:08:26.780
That is massive.

00:08:26.780 --> 00:08:27.660
It is.

00:08:27.660 --> 00:08:32.620
Which means CMS is shifting the entire focus of the star ratings away from the process

00:08:32.620 --> 00:08:35.820
and heavily toward long-term preventative outcomes.

00:08:35.820 --> 00:08:39.820
It's no longer enough to prove you have a polite call center or short wait times.

00:08:39.820 --> 00:08:42.140
You can't just prove you scheduled a screening.

00:08:42.140 --> 00:08:44.620
The plan has to prove, mathematically,

00:08:44.620 --> 00:08:47.900
that its members are actually staying physically and mentally healthier

00:08:47.900 --> 00:08:49.100
over a long period of time.

00:08:49.100 --> 00:08:49.580
Exactly.

00:08:50.140 --> 00:08:52.780
Knowing that the test got substantially harder

00:08:52.780 --> 00:08:56.140
and that it now requires this intense real-time data monitoring,

00:08:56.700 --> 00:09:00.780
you would naturally expect the traditionally highest performing types of organizations

00:09:00.780 --> 00:09:02.140
to dominate those top spots.

00:09:02.700 --> 00:09:05.980
But the CMS fact sheet reveals a massive anomaly here.

00:09:06.700 --> 00:09:10.700
Let's look at the split between non-profit and for-profit health plans.

00:09:10.700 --> 00:09:12.940
What's fascinating here is historically,

00:09:12.940 --> 00:09:16.540
and even in this new strict 2027 environment,

00:09:16.540 --> 00:09:21.820
non-profit organizations generally score much better across the broad average.

00:09:21.820 --> 00:09:24.540
The CMS data explicitly points this out.

00:09:24.540 --> 00:09:30.380
Approximately 44% of non-profit contracts received four or more stars this year.

00:09:30.380 --> 00:09:32.540
Right. Compared to the for-profit plans,

00:09:32.540 --> 00:09:35.820
where only 34% managed to hit that four-star mark.

00:09:36.540 --> 00:09:40.700
So if you're just playing the odds and you pick a non-profit plan out of a hat,

00:09:40.700 --> 00:09:44.140
it is statistically more likely to be a higher rated plan.

00:09:44.140 --> 00:09:46.780
That holds true for the middle of the pack, absolutely.

00:09:46.780 --> 00:09:50.060
But when we look at the absolute peak of the mountain,

00:09:50.060 --> 00:09:54.700
those elite 15 plans that managed to secure the perfect five-star rating,

00:09:54.700 --> 00:09:57.420
a completely different narrative emerges.

00:09:57.420 --> 00:10:02.620
Out of those 15 plans nationwide, 14 of them are for-profit carriers.

00:10:02.620 --> 00:10:04.460
14 out of 15.

00:10:04.460 --> 00:10:05.340
Yes.

00:10:05.340 --> 00:10:09.100
The broad average is totally dominated by non-profits,

00:10:09.100 --> 00:10:13.740
but the absolute peak of performance is almost exclusively for-profit.

00:10:14.780 --> 00:10:17.500
Let me throw a theory at you, because when I read that,

00:10:18.140 --> 00:10:20.140
my first thought went straight to resources.

00:10:21.180 --> 00:10:26.060
Are we seeing a scenario where it now takes massive for-profit corporate bank accounts

00:10:26.060 --> 00:10:29.260
to buy the sophisticated data analytics required

00:10:29.260 --> 00:10:32.220
to track these complex new CMS metrics?

00:10:32.220 --> 00:10:34.460
Like they're just buying their way to the top.

00:10:34.460 --> 00:10:34.860
Exactly.

00:10:35.580 --> 00:10:38.460
Are the non-profits just getting stuck at four-stars

00:10:38.460 --> 00:10:42.300
because they simply can't afford the multi-million-dollar software suites

00:10:42.300 --> 00:10:43.340
needed to monitor, say,

00:10:44.220 --> 00:10:46.780
anticholinergic overlaps in real time?

00:10:46.780 --> 00:10:48.620
I'm going to push back on that, actually.

00:10:49.420 --> 00:10:51.660
Because while that is a really common assumption,

00:10:51.660 --> 00:10:55.980
it kind of misses the structural reality of these organizations.

00:10:56.060 --> 00:10:58.620
It's not just about the size of the bank account.

00:10:59.260 --> 00:11:01.900
Some of the largest non-profit health systems in the country

00:11:01.900 --> 00:11:05.340
sit on massive multi-billion-dollar endowments.

00:11:05.340 --> 00:11:05.820
Oh, right.

00:11:06.540 --> 00:11:07.580
Good point.

00:11:07.580 --> 00:11:13.980
The real issue dividing the four-star plans from the five-star plans is bureaucratic agility.

00:11:13.980 --> 00:11:14.540
Agility.

00:11:15.100 --> 00:11:15.820
Explain that.

00:11:15.820 --> 00:11:16.140
Okay.

00:11:16.140 --> 00:11:20.700
So achieving a four-star rating requires delivering consistently good care,

00:11:21.500 --> 00:11:25.340
but achieving a five-star rating under the new 2027 rules

00:11:25.980 --> 00:11:31.340
requires flawless data capture and the ability to pivot your entire operational structure

00:11:31.340 --> 00:11:32.780
incredibly rapidly.

00:11:33.420 --> 00:11:35.020
Ah, I see where you're going with this.

00:11:35.660 --> 00:11:35.980
Right.

00:11:35.980 --> 00:11:41.020
A massive for-profit entity can often make a unilateral executive decision to

00:11:41.020 --> 00:11:43.580
scrap their old tech stack, hire a new vendor,

00:11:43.580 --> 00:11:47.900
and completely rebuild their population health analytics in a single fiscal year

00:11:47.900 --> 00:11:49.580
just to meet a new CMS metric.

00:11:50.220 --> 00:11:55.900
Whereas a traditional non-profit might require three years of committee reviews

00:11:55.900 --> 00:12:00.060
board approvals and pilot programs just to change how they text patients about flu shots.

00:12:00.940 --> 00:12:01.420
Exactly.

00:12:01.420 --> 00:12:03.660
It's about how fast they can adapt to the new math.

00:12:04.380 --> 00:12:04.620
Okay.

00:12:04.620 --> 00:12:05.500
That makes a lot of sense.

00:12:06.140 --> 00:12:10.460
The for-profits have the structural agility to pivot the entire machine

00:12:10.460 --> 00:12:12.700
when CMS suddenly changes the grading rubric.

00:12:13.580 --> 00:12:19.180
But even with that agility, as we saw, only 14 for-profit plans in the entire country

00:12:19.180 --> 00:12:20.380
actually pulled it off.

00:12:20.380 --> 00:12:24.460
Which brings us to the most fascinating geographical data in this entire deep dive.

00:12:24.460 --> 00:12:28.700
So if limitless corporate wealth isn't the guaranteed silver bullet to a five-star rating,

00:12:28.700 --> 00:12:30.220
what actually is?

00:12:30.220 --> 00:12:33.820
To answer that, we have to look at the map of these 15 elite winners

00:12:33.820 --> 00:12:37.980
because there is a massive cluster in a place you might least expect.

00:12:37.980 --> 00:12:40.540
Here's where it gets really interesting.

00:12:40.540 --> 00:12:43.100
When you look at the Becker's Payer Issues breakdown,

00:12:43.100 --> 00:12:45.500
you see a scattering of winners across the mainland.

00:12:45.500 --> 00:12:49.340
You have plans in New York, Florida, Michigan, Nevada.

00:12:49.340 --> 00:12:51.500
But then you look at Puerto Rico.

00:12:51.500 --> 00:12:54.380
Out of the 15 elite five-star plans nationwide,

00:12:54.700 --> 00:12:57.020
two of them are based in Puerto Rico.

00:12:57.020 --> 00:13:00.300
Yeah, let's establish exactly who these standout organizations are.

00:13:00.300 --> 00:13:02.380
First, we have MCS Classicare.

00:13:02.380 --> 00:13:05.420
This is a for-profit plan under MHH Healthcare.

00:13:05.420 --> 00:13:08.540
And according to the local reporting in El Nuevo Día,

00:13:08.540 --> 00:13:12.060
they just achieved five stars for the fourth consecutive year.

00:13:12.060 --> 00:13:13.260
Four years in a row.

00:13:13.260 --> 00:13:13.740
Right.

00:13:13.740 --> 00:13:17.740
They are the only plan on the island to maintain this maximum score year after year,

00:13:17.740 --> 00:13:22.460
which, given everything we just discussed about how much harder CMS made the test,

00:13:22.460 --> 00:13:25.420
is just a staggering logistical achievement.

00:13:25.420 --> 00:13:27.020
Then we have Triple-S Advantage,

00:13:27.020 --> 00:13:30.300
which is a for-profit plan operating under GuideWell.

00:13:30.300 --> 00:13:34.060
According to Sin Comillas, they hit five stars for their HMO contract

00:13:34.060 --> 00:13:37.500
and 4.5 stars for their PPO contract this year.

00:13:37.500 --> 00:13:40.540
Let's quickly differentiate those for the listener just in case.

00:13:40.540 --> 00:13:45.900
An HMO or Health Maintenance Organization generally requires patients to use a strict,

00:13:45.900 --> 00:13:50.380
localized network of doctors and get referrals from a primary care physician.

00:13:50.380 --> 00:13:53.100
It's highly controlled and highly coordinated.

00:13:53.100 --> 00:13:53.900
Right.

00:13:53.900 --> 00:13:56.620
A PPO, or preferred provider organization,

00:13:56.620 --> 00:14:00.700
offers more flexibility to see out-of-network specialists without a referral,

00:14:00.700 --> 00:14:03.580
but that flexibility makes it much harder for the health plan to track

00:14:03.580 --> 00:14:05.420
every single thing the patient is doing.

00:14:05.420 --> 00:14:09.340
Which perfectly explains why Triple-S scored the flawless five stars

00:14:09.340 --> 00:14:11.260
on their highly coordinated HMO,

00:14:11.260 --> 00:14:13.900
but slightly lower on their more flexible PPO.

00:14:13.900 --> 00:14:18.220
But to truly grasp the weight of what Puerto Rico has achieved here,

00:14:18.220 --> 00:14:20.220
we have to look beyond just the star ratings

00:14:20.220 --> 00:14:23.100
and look at the sheer scale of the success.

00:14:23.100 --> 00:14:24.220
The volume of patients.

00:14:24.220 --> 00:14:25.180
Exactly.

00:14:25.180 --> 00:14:27.660
The enrollment numbers are where this story stops

00:14:27.660 --> 00:14:30.060
being just an interesting regional footnote

00:14:30.060 --> 00:14:33.260
and becomes a complete disruption of the national health care narrative.

00:14:33.900 --> 00:14:37.660
Let's do the math on this because when I laid out the Becker's data side by side,

00:14:37.660 --> 00:14:39.340
it is almost unbelievable.

00:14:40.060 --> 00:14:42.620
We are looking at a list of the 15 best plans in America.

