And if you aggregate all of those mainland five-star enrollees together,

00:15:17.180 --> 00:15:24.540
you get roughly 305,000 patients across the entire continental United States receiving

00:15:24.540 --> 00:15:25.740
five-star care.

00:15:25.740 --> 00:15:27.900
Okay, now let's look at Puerto Rico.

00:15:27.900 --> 00:15:33.980
MCS Classicare alone has an astronomical 360,519 enrollees.

00:15:34.540 --> 00:15:40.060
Triple-S Advantage has 120,943 on their five-star contract.

00:15:40.060 --> 00:15:45.820
That is over 481,000 patients in Puerto Rico receiving the absolute highest

00:15:45.820 --> 00:15:47.420
federally rated care available.

00:15:47.980 --> 00:15:50.460
The contrast is just staggering.

00:15:50.460 --> 00:15:51.100
It is.

00:15:51.100 --> 00:15:56.700
The two plans in Puerto Rico have more patients enrolled in five-star Medicare Advantage Care

00:15:56.700 --> 00:16:00.140
than the entire rest of the United States combined.

00:16:00.140 --> 00:16:01.500
That blew my mind.

00:16:01.500 --> 00:16:06.700
The vast majority of all five-star patients in America right now are living on one island.

00:16:07.340 --> 00:16:10.380
And when you factor in the broader 4.5-star ratings,

00:16:10.380 --> 00:16:15.740
nearly 100% of Medicare Advantage patients in Puerto Rico are receiving top-tier care.

00:16:15.740 --> 00:16:17.740
If we connect this to the bigger picture,

00:16:17.740 --> 00:16:21.580
it creates what the industry views as a profound healthcare paradox.

00:16:22.140 --> 00:16:26.700
Puerto Rico is operating under some of the most challenging economic conditions in the

00:16:26.700 --> 00:16:28.620
entire U.S. healthcare system.

00:16:28.620 --> 00:16:32.860
They historically face lower federal reimbursement rates than mainland states.

00:16:32.860 --> 00:16:34.860
And complex infrastructure challenges, right?

00:16:34.860 --> 00:16:35.580
Absolutely.

00:16:35.580 --> 00:16:39.180
They are operating in an environment of severe economic stress,

00:16:39.180 --> 00:16:43.660
dealing with hurdles that wealthy mainland jurisdictions simply do not have to manage.

00:16:43.660 --> 00:16:47.340
Which completely destroys the excuse that you need a wealthy zip code to deliver

00:16:47.340 --> 00:16:48.540
perfect healthcare.

00:16:48.540 --> 00:16:51.900
I mean, if Puerto Rico is hitting 100% on a harder test

00:16:51.900 --> 00:16:53.820
while operating with fewer resources,

00:16:54.460 --> 00:16:58.380
then mainland plans don't really have an excuse for their dropping scores, do they?

00:16:58.380 --> 00:16:59.100
They really don't.

00:16:59.660 --> 00:17:03.020
And this is exactly why healthcare administrators across the country

00:17:03.020 --> 00:17:05.900
are studying what MCS and Triple-S are doing.

00:17:05.900 --> 00:17:08.540
Because their success is clearly not just a product

00:17:08.540 --> 00:17:11.740
of having unlimited cash to throw at software vendors.

00:17:11.740 --> 00:17:15.580
It is a fundamental structural difference in how they deliver care.

00:17:15.580 --> 00:17:18.300
In the local reporting, we actually have quotes from the leadership

00:17:18.300 --> 00:17:21.340
of both organizations pointing directly to their strategy.

00:17:21.420 --> 00:17:24.780
Dr. Inés Hernández, the chief medical officer at MCS,

00:17:24.780 --> 00:17:29.180
told El Nuevo Día that this sustained excellence proves they can offer the

00:17:29.180 --> 00:17:31.580
highest levels of execution, quote,

00:17:31.580 --> 00:17:35.100
even under the most challenging economic conditions in the United States.

00:17:35.820 --> 00:17:36.300
Yeah.

00:17:36.300 --> 00:17:38.700
And Jim O'Drobinak, the CEO of MCS,

00:17:38.700 --> 00:17:42.300
emphasized establishing the best alliances with providers on the island.

00:17:43.180 --> 00:17:47.660
Over triple-S, CEO Michael A. Muchniki told Sin Comillas

00:17:47.660 --> 00:17:49.980
that their five-star rating comes down to the strength of their

00:17:49.980 --> 00:17:53.100
relationships with physicians and community organizations,

00:17:53.100 --> 00:17:56.060
basically making care more coordinated and responsive.

00:17:56.060 --> 00:17:59.420
Now, at first glance, that might sound like standard corporate PR,

00:17:59.420 --> 00:18:02.380
you know, every health plan claims to have great relationships

00:18:02.380 --> 00:18:03.820
with their doctors and communities.

00:18:03.820 --> 00:18:04.700
Oh, absolutely.

00:18:04.700 --> 00:18:07.580
We love our community as plastered on every corporate brochure.

00:18:08.220 --> 00:18:11.740
But a friendly relationship doesn't automatically fulfill a complex CMS

00:18:11.740 --> 00:18:13.260
metric about opioid usage.

00:18:13.900 --> 00:18:17.900
How does an alliance actually translate into a perfect federal math score?

00:18:18.540 --> 00:18:22.780
The mechanism here is what we call the vertical integration of community resources.

00:18:23.420 --> 00:18:26.780
Mainland plans often rely on top-down software approaches.

00:18:26.780 --> 00:18:31.020
They buy an expensive analytics program that flags a missed screening,

00:18:31.020 --> 00:18:34.620
and the software automatically sends a text message or an email to the patient.

00:18:34.620 --> 00:18:36.860
But a text message is so easy to ignore,

00:18:36.860 --> 00:18:39.020
especially for older, vulnerable populations.

00:18:39.020 --> 00:18:39.900
Exactly.

00:18:39.900 --> 00:18:41.500
The dashboard flashes a warning light,

00:18:41.500 --> 00:18:43.740
but nobody is actually fixing the engine.

00:18:43.740 --> 00:18:44.940
Ah, right.

00:18:44.940 --> 00:18:48.220
What Puerto Rico has mastered is using human infrastructure

00:18:48.220 --> 00:18:51.340
as their primary data capture and intervention tool.

00:18:51.340 --> 00:18:55.980
An integrated alliance means the health plan might fund local community health workers.

00:18:55.980 --> 00:18:57.580
When a patient misses an appointment,

00:18:57.580 --> 00:19:00.220
or when a high-risk medication is prescribed,

00:19:00.220 --> 00:19:02.140
it isn't just an automated email.

00:19:02.140 --> 00:19:04.540
A local, culturally competent community worker

00:19:04.540 --> 00:19:06.620
might physically go to the patient's home.

00:19:06.620 --> 00:19:08.620
Wait, like physically knock on their door?

00:19:08.620 --> 00:19:09.420
Yes.

00:19:09.420 --> 00:19:11.820
They sit at the kitchen table, look at the pill bottles,

00:19:11.820 --> 00:19:15.660
and realize, oh, the patient is taking an opioid from their surgeon,

00:19:15.660 --> 00:19:18.140
and a benzodiazepine from their psychiatrist.

00:19:18.140 --> 00:19:18.860
Wow.

00:19:18.860 --> 00:19:22.060
So they are bypassing the entirely fragmented digital systems

00:19:22.060 --> 00:19:24.220
by putting actual eyes directly on the patient.

00:19:24.220 --> 00:19:25.660
They intervene right there.

00:19:25.660 --> 00:19:29.260
The community worker coordinates directly with the primary care doctor

00:19:29.260 --> 00:19:32.220
and the pharmacist to fix the prescription overlap.

00:19:32.220 --> 00:19:35.180
They ensure the patient actually attends the preventative screening

00:19:35.180 --> 00:19:37.260
by, you know, arranging transportation.

00:19:37.260 --> 00:19:41.180
They are physically forcing the outcomes that CMS is demanding.

00:19:41.180 --> 00:19:44.540
When CMS triples the weight of improving physical and mental health,

00:19:44.540 --> 00:19:48.540
you cannot fake that with good paperwork or a fancy software algorithm.

00:19:48.540 --> 00:19:50.380
The patient actually has to be healthier.

00:19:51.180 --> 00:19:53.980
It's active, localized engagement.

00:19:54.780 --> 00:19:59.180
The CEO of MCS even stated that their goal is to go beyond being a plan

00:19:59.180 --> 00:20:00.700
that simply pays for your care,

00:20:01.420 --> 00:20:03.740
to being a plan that actively occupies itself

00:20:03.740 --> 00:20:05.340
with keeping Puerto Rico healthy.

00:20:05.900 --> 00:20:08.300
They are taking direct on-the-ground ownership

00:20:08.300 --> 00:20:09.980
of the population's daily outcomes.

00:20:10.780 --> 00:20:12.540
And the results validate the model.

00:20:12.540 --> 00:20:14.140
You have to remember the baseline here.

00:20:14.700 --> 00:20:19.980
71% of all MA enrollees nationwide are in contracts with four or more stars.

00:20:20.540 --> 00:20:23.260
But in Puerto Rico, you have this massive concentration

00:20:23.260 --> 00:20:26.620
of nearly half a million people sitting at the absolute pinnacle,

00:20:26.620 --> 00:20:27.900
the five-star level.

00:20:27.900 --> 00:20:28.540
It's incredible.

00:20:29.100 --> 00:20:31.100
By deeply aligning the health plan's goals

00:20:31.100 --> 00:20:33.980
with the primary care doctors and local community groups,

00:20:33.980 --> 00:20:37.260
they have built a system that is far more efficient at capturing data

00:20:37.260 --> 00:20:39.100
and driving healthy behavior

00:20:39.100 --> 00:20:42.620
than a disconnected mainland system relying purely on technology.

00:20:42.620 --> 00:20:45.420
So what does this all mean for you, the listener?

00:20:45.420 --> 00:20:46.780
Let's bring this all together.

00:20:47.420 --> 00:20:50.940
The big takeaway is that the CMS ratings are getting much stricter,

00:20:50.940 --> 00:20:52.940
